{
  "image_name": "179382c1b6a93c93d2c8cec6db2c907e.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a biomass-to-products biorefinery pathway centered on lignocellulosic plant material. It begins with biomass grown using sunlight and CO2, shown as plant feedstocks whose microstructure contains cellulose, hemicellulose, and lignin. A pretreatment step, with examples such as acid, AFEX, and organosolv, separates or loosens these components so they can be processed further.\n\nThe main conversion route then depolymerizes the polymers into smaller molecules. The diagram highlights cleavage of lignin, hemicellulose, and cellulose, and indicates both enzymatic and abiotic approaches. Notes beside these steps emphasize practical constraints such as enzyme stability and activity, expression or enzyme cocktails, and compatibility of chemical methods with downstream fermentation. Engineering is presented as a cross-cutting tool, including cell, pathway, and enzyme engineering.\n\nAfter depolymerization, fermentation converts the released intermediates into useful outputs. The products branch into commodity chemicals, short-lived products such as biofuels and biodegradable plastics, and long-lived bioplastic products. Example chemicals listed include ethanol, succinic acid, lactic acid, ethylene glycol, and xylitol. Overall, the figure conveys an integrated circular bioeconomy in which renewable biomass is upgraded into chemicals and materials, including higher-performance materials, while linking product use back to the carbon cycle.",
